00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:56.610
GWEN SHAPIRA: So basically we
say that Kafka is a streaming

00:01:56.610 --> 00:01:57.180
platform.

00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:58.750
What does it actually mean?

00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:01.170
It means that you
can produce events.

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:05.430
Kafka will store them as an
ordered stream of events.

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:06.990
It will maintain this order.

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:09.030
And then you can
consume those events.

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:11.647
And you can even consume
them, do stuff to them,

00:02:11.647 --> 00:02:13.230
and write them back
to Kafka, which is

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:16.830
what we call stream processing.

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:20.200
Just to show you what stream
processing could look like--

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:25.470
imagine someone trying to
bring a credit card processor.

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:28.470
So you get a lot of
authorization attempts

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:31.170
from credit cards every day--
like thousands and thousands

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:32.100
every second.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:35.700
A tiny percentage of them
could be incredibly suspicious.

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:37.980
So we need to detect
the suspicious events

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:39.810
and separate them
from the good events.

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:41.850
And basically have
someone investigate

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:44.290
them a bit more, right?

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:46.290
And it sounds like it
could be very complicated.

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:49.450
To be honest, doing it in real
life is pretty complicated.

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:52.290
But it can start with
something incredibly simple.

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:54.720
If someone is trying
to repeatedly authorize

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:56.250
the same card over
and over again,

00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:58.500
it's probably very suspicious.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:01.440
So we want to basically
filter the stream

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:04.830
of authorization attempts
for this kind of behavior.

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:06.330
And create a new
stream of things

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:07.889
that may possibly be wrong.

00:03:07.889 --> 00:03:09.930
And we want to do it every
time an event happens.

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:11.110
We want to do it continuously.

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:12.651
That's the core of
stream processing.

00:03:12.651 --> 00:03:16.020
Not just once a day because
then the bad guys may be already

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:17.670
all the way back to somewhere.

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:19.080
And who knows.

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:20.380
We may never catch them again.

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:23.290
So we want to
continuously process it.

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:27.510
And as you can see, using
KSQL is the simplest way

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:29.580
to do stream processing
even if you know almost

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:31.762
nothing about streams or Java.

00:03:31.762 --> 00:03:32.470
JAY SMITH: Right.

00:03:32.470 --> 00:03:34.920
This looks very similar
to your standard SQL.

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:36.644
Maybe a few little differences.

00:03:36.644 --> 00:03:37.560
GWEN SHAPIRA: Exactly.

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:40.620
So just to show you how it
works, we basically say OK,

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:44.250
we're creating a new
stream of possible frauds

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:48.690
based on an existing stream
of authorization attempts.

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:51.745
And the way we
select possible fraud

00:03:51.745 --> 00:03:53.370
is that we look at
five-minute windows.

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:57.210
So we cut down the stream
into five minutes windows.

00:03:57.210 --> 00:04:01.240
Pick the card number and count
the authorization attempts

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:04.650
of this card in this window.

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:06.760
And then say everything
with more than three

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:09.900
in a five-minute window
looks like a suspicious card.

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:11.640
And we're writing it
as possible fraud.

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:13.290
And then we have
an ongoing stream

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:15.090
of things that look suspicious.

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:17.519
Now we want to basically
send it somewhere else

00:04:17.519 --> 00:04:20.760
for deeper analysis, where
maybe a qualified human can

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:22.453
do extra investigation.

00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:28.610
So really, the core
idea of streaming

00:04:28.610 --> 00:04:31.790
is that you don't process
events once in a while,

00:04:31.790 --> 00:04:35.910
you process them as they're
going on in real time.

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:37.830
If you learn nothing
else about processing,

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:41.670
that is the concept
you have to remember.

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:42.570
So how do we do it?

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:47.640
So let's look at the components
that make up this platform.

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:50.080
And the core component
is the Kafka brokers

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:53.900
or what we also call a
log or a distributed log.

00:04:53.900 --> 00:04:56.850
There is a famous book
by my CEO, Jay Kreps,

00:04:56.850 --> 00:04:58.710
called "I Love Logs"
where he basically

00:04:58.710 --> 00:05:01.740
explains that log is the
fundamental data structure

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:03.790
of all modern computing.

00:05:03.790 --> 00:05:06.720
And it turns out that logs
are incredibly simple.

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:10.080
It is a data structure where
you write things at the end

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:12.750
and you store the
structure in order

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:16.290
for a set amount of time,
which could be forever.

00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:20.650
Some people do hundreds
of years amount of time.

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:25.050
And you can see that this
could be your application log,

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:27.540
this can be a click stream,
this can be a credit card

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:28.890
authorization attempt.

00:05:28.890 --> 00:05:31.770
The message is so generic.

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:36.910
Like an event is-- anything
that has happened could be it.

00:05:36.910 --> 00:05:39.160
And you keep writing
events at the end

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:41.900
and then different
applications want to read it.

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:44.500
And the idea is that every
application is independent.

00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:47.170
And they start at some
point, usually the beginning.

00:05:47.170 --> 00:05:48.830
And they continue
in one direction,

00:05:48.830 --> 00:05:50.340
hopefully to the end.

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:52.720
And there is one
direction of movement,

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:55.900
which makes it just efficient
on disks and this kind of stuff.

00:05:55.900 --> 00:05:58.630
And also each one of
those consumers only

00:05:58.630 --> 00:06:02.350
needs to know OK, the last
message I got is message five.

00:06:02.350 --> 00:06:04.070
The next one is going
to be message six.

00:06:04.070 --> 00:06:07.450
Let me ping the broker and
say, can I get message six?

00:06:07.450 --> 00:06:09.840
Can I get message seven?

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:11.830
You get a bit more than
just the next message.

00:06:11.830 --> 00:06:13.130
But that's the idea.

00:06:13.130 --> 00:06:14.980
Which means that the
clients and a broker

00:06:14.980 --> 00:06:18.010
don't need to keep track
of every single event.

00:06:18.010 --> 00:06:19.360
And did everyone consume it?

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:20.917
And who is supposed
to consume it?

00:06:20.917 --> 00:06:22.750
This is one of the
things that we are always

00:06:22.750 --> 00:06:25.330
a bit more
computationally-intensive and

00:06:25.330 --> 00:06:27.640
memory-intensive in
all the message bases

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:29.039
and really set Kafka apart.

00:06:29.039 --> 00:06:30.580
JAY SMITH: So since
its all in order,

00:06:30.580 --> 00:06:32.290
its easier to keep track of.

00:06:32.290 --> 00:06:33.040
GWEN SHAPIRA: Yes.

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:33.290
Exactly.

00:06:33.290 --> 00:06:35.620
You just have to remember
one number per consumer.

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:38.110
So sometimes people ask
me how many consumers can

00:06:38.110 --> 00:06:39.460
I have with Kafka?

00:06:39.460 --> 00:06:41.807
And I know 2,000.

00:06:41.807 --> 00:06:43.090
50,000.

00:06:43.090 --> 00:06:44.170
It's just one number.

00:06:44.170 --> 00:06:45.730
How much does it long take?

00:06:45.730 --> 00:06:46.230
Right?

00:06:49.850 --> 00:06:53.090
And then I just showed you
one log, which in Kafka terms

00:06:53.090 --> 00:06:54.500
is one partition.

00:06:54.500 --> 00:06:57.960
A Kafka topic is made up
of a lot of partitions.

00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:00.320
I think the topic is kind
of this logical entity--

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:02.110
maybe like that base table--

00:07:02.110 --> 00:07:06.160
then Kafka is made up of
a lot of those topics.

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:09.380
I'm going to show you a lot
of topics when we do the demo.

00:07:09.380 --> 00:07:11.270
But that the idea is
that the topic will

00:07:11.270 --> 00:07:12.170
have many partitions.

00:07:12.170 --> 00:07:13.935
You can spread them
across many machines.

00:07:13.935 --> 00:07:16.160
You can have many
producers writing to them.

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:17.630
Many consumers reading.

00:07:17.630 --> 00:07:22.360
So it's really a
scalable system.

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:24.870
The other thing is that each
partition is also replicated,

00:07:24.870 --> 00:07:26.370
usually on streamed machines.

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:27.480
So you have a leader.

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:29.520
All reads and writes
go to the leader.

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:31.110
And then their
followers basically

00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:33.080
keep replicating syncs.

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:34.110
You have one job.

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:35.920
Keep replicating it.

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:38.370
And if something
happens to the leader,

00:07:38.370 --> 00:07:40.830
basically, one of the
replicas take over.

00:07:40.830 --> 00:07:43.430
The idea is that because
all reads and writes

00:07:43.430 --> 00:07:46.110
have been leader, we always
have full consistency

00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:47.100
on that partition.

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:51.450
Which is really a core
guarantee that Kafka has.

00:07:51.450 --> 00:07:52.710
JAY SMITH: That's good.

00:07:52.710 --> 00:07:56.400
I mean, if you have all of those
events and one of the machines

00:07:56.400 --> 00:07:57.840
goes down, heaven forbid--

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:01.060
you want to make sure you
can maintain those events.

00:08:01.060 --> 00:08:04.544
So having this replica
technology built in to Kafka--

00:08:04.544 --> 00:08:05.460
GWEN SHAPIRA: Exactly.

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:07.040
And usually when
things go wrong,

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:08.940
it's because people
misconfigure replication.

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:11.190
And think that they have
guarantees that they actually

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:13.550
don't.

00:08:13.550 --> 00:08:15.050
So you can see
here's that it's kind

00:08:15.050 --> 00:08:18.090
of really scalable
end-to-end-- from producers

00:08:18.090 --> 00:08:20.000
through partitions to consumers.

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:22.890
And consumers really have
the idea of consumer group.

00:08:22.890 --> 00:08:26.830
So if one consumer instance
cannot handle all the data from

00:08:26.830 --> 00:08:30.470
all the partitions because
thousands of credit cards per

00:08:30.470 --> 00:08:33.740
second is a lot of work, you
can actually start multiple

00:08:33.740 --> 00:08:36.289
instances and belong to
the same consumer group.

00:08:36.289 --> 00:08:38.990
And they will basically
negotiate with each other

00:08:38.990 --> 00:08:41.720
and say, OK, this one is
reading this partition,

00:08:41.720 --> 00:08:43.760
the other one is reading
the other partition.

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:46.670
And you get scalability on
the consumer applications.

00:08:46.670 --> 00:08:49.430
And also high-availability
if one of those crashes,

00:08:49.430 --> 00:08:52.910
basically the partitions will
get reassigned to someone else.

00:08:52.910 --> 00:08:58.000
So it's really scalable
and resilient end-to-end.

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:00.550
And then if you go to the
Apache-Kafka projects,

00:09:00.550 --> 00:09:03.000
you'll only really get
clients in two languages--

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:04.810
the Java and Scala.

00:09:04.810 --> 00:09:09.444
But Kafka has a binary protocol
that is only slightly complex.

00:09:09.444 --> 00:09:11.110
So let's look inside
of the implementing

00:09:11.110 --> 00:09:13.640
this binary protocol
in different languages.

00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:15.856
So you have a
pretty big ecosystem

00:09:15.856 --> 00:09:17.230
in all those
different languages.

00:09:17.230 --> 00:09:19.021
And then if your language
is not supported,

00:09:19.021 --> 00:09:22.240
you have arrest proxies
that will give you support.

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:23.879
Pretty much everyone
does REST, right?

00:09:23.879 --> 00:09:24.670
JAY SMITH: Exactly.

00:09:24.670 --> 00:09:26.834
And that's great
because a lot of times

00:09:26.834 --> 00:09:28.250
when you want to
learn a new tool,

00:09:28.250 --> 00:09:31.880
there's a limitation
based on whatever software

00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:34.120
or whatever language
it supports.

00:09:34.120 --> 00:09:37.330
But being able to support
a wide array of languages,

00:09:37.330 --> 00:09:39.490
I can just take whatever
knowledge I have today

00:09:39.490 --> 00:09:42.997
and take advantage
of the REST proxy.

00:09:42.997 --> 00:09:44.580
GWEN SHAPIRA: Do you
notice that we're

00:09:44.580 --> 00:09:47.217
expected to learn new languages
faster than we did in the past?

00:09:47.217 --> 00:09:48.050
JAY SMITH: Oh, yeah.

00:09:48.050 --> 00:09:50.266
There's like three new ones
every month or something.

00:09:50.266 --> 00:09:51.140
GWEN SHAPIRA: I know.

00:09:51.140 --> 00:09:53.800
I spent most of my
career with Java.

00:09:53.800 --> 00:09:56.830
And then suddenly it's like,
oh, I have to learn GO.

00:09:56.830 --> 00:09:59.780
And now I was told that
Trust is the really big deal.

00:09:59.780 --> 00:10:01.990
And [INAUDIBLE]
is a really big--

00:10:01.990 --> 00:10:04.120
Everything keeps happening.

00:10:04.120 --> 00:10:07.060
And it's funny, I have a friend
who is now learning REST.

00:10:07.060 --> 00:10:09.550
And he is like, do you think
I can just write a Kafka

00:10:09.550 --> 00:10:11.740
producer as my first project?

00:10:11.740 --> 00:10:15.580
And I'm like, go for it, man.

00:10:15.580 --> 00:10:18.280
JAY SMITH: Maybe he'll
write one in Julia too now.

00:10:18.280 --> 00:10:20.665
GWEN SHAPIRA: Ooh, maybe
if you have spare time.

00:10:20.665 --> 00:10:22.260
I don't know, do
you do hack projects

00:10:22.260 --> 00:10:23.530
and that kind of thing?

00:10:23.530 --> 00:10:24.040
JAY SMITH: Sure, why not?

00:10:24.040 --> 00:10:25.540
GWEN SHAPIRA: Yeah,
20% time, right?

00:10:25.540 --> 00:10:26.050
That's the--

00:10:28.830 --> 00:10:31.240
So until now, I
kind of explained

00:10:31.240 --> 00:10:34.030
how you produce and consume
events from your applications

00:10:34.030 --> 00:10:35.680
that you coded yourself.

00:10:35.680 --> 00:10:38.770
But there is a lot of cases
where the data you want

00:10:38.770 --> 00:10:40.960
is not something that you
actually create yourself.

00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:44.050
It actually already
exists in some database.

00:10:44.050 --> 00:10:45.634
And you can't really
ask the person

00:10:45.634 --> 00:10:47.050
writing to do their
best to please

00:10:47.050 --> 00:10:49.880
also write to Kafka because
they have their own priorities.

00:10:49.880 --> 00:10:52.330
Well, you can really
connect to the database

00:10:52.330 --> 00:10:55.054
and get events to Kafka
with the Connect framework.

00:10:55.054 --> 00:10:57.220
I mean, you could write it
yourself in your own app.

00:10:57.220 --> 00:10:59.170
But there is so much
in common between all

00:10:59.170 --> 00:11:01.944
those different
pull-and-produce loops

00:11:01.944 --> 00:11:04.360
that we figured that we may
as well give you the framework

00:11:04.360 --> 00:11:08.180
and let you focus on how to
query the Azure database.

00:11:08.180 --> 00:11:10.140
So it's a very basic idea.

00:11:10.140 --> 00:11:13.230
Did you get that from a database
into Kafka, or from Kafka

00:11:13.230 --> 00:11:13.910
into a database?

00:11:13.910 --> 00:11:15.630
You don't have to
do the entire flow.

00:11:15.630 --> 00:11:17.620
Just half of it is fine.

00:11:17.620 --> 00:11:21.490
But you know, then we'll
show you the entire flow.

00:11:21.490 --> 00:11:24.010
And obviously once
you write a good API,

00:11:24.010 --> 00:11:26.000
communities are kind of amazing.

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:29.410
So hundreds of
Connect-ers showed up

00:11:29.410 --> 00:11:31.960
from all those different
teams and companies

00:11:31.960 --> 00:11:35.980
like the connector from
Kafka to Google BigQuery

00:11:35.980 --> 00:11:39.094
was written by a
company called WePay.

00:11:39.094 --> 00:11:40.510
And they just the
open-sourced it.

00:11:40.510 --> 00:11:43.000
And now 10 other
companies also use it,

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:45.600
because apparently getting
data from Kafka to BigQuery

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:46.750
is a very big thing.

00:11:46.750 --> 00:11:47.680
JAY SMITH: Right.

00:11:47.680 --> 00:11:50.980
Apache Kafka, we must
emphasize the fact

00:11:50.980 --> 00:11:52.400
that it is open source.

00:11:52.400 --> 00:11:54.460
It is very community-driven.

00:11:54.460 --> 00:11:57.100
Confluent is very active
in that community.

00:11:57.100 --> 00:12:01.000
So you do get a lot of
these third-party connectors

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:03.850
and tools built for it due
to its open-source nature.

00:12:03.850 --> 00:12:05.020
GWEN SHAPIRA: Exactly.

00:12:05.020 --> 00:12:07.780
And for me, it's just always
amazing the creativity

00:12:07.780 --> 00:12:09.080
of the community.

00:12:09.080 --> 00:12:11.890
Did you ever want to get events
from a Bloomberg terminal

00:12:11.890 --> 00:12:13.360
into an IRC channel,
for example?

00:12:16.600 --> 00:12:18.190
And then the last
component is really

00:12:18.190 --> 00:12:19.450
doing with the
stream processing.

00:12:19.450 --> 00:12:22.074
And I kind of already showed you
how it works in the beginning.

00:12:22.074 --> 00:12:24.320
So I don't have to
go over that again.

00:12:24.320 --> 00:12:27.730
But I wanted to mention that
this is kind of friendly SQL,

00:12:27.730 --> 00:12:32.715
but its a layer on top of
a Java API and a Scala API

00:12:32.715 --> 00:12:35.560
is that you can really
use more directly if you

00:12:35.560 --> 00:12:38.920
prefer using Java and Scala--
which a lot of us really do.

00:12:38.920 --> 00:12:41.680
And it does give you a bit
more power and flexibility

00:12:41.680 --> 00:12:44.200
at the expense of
slightly more complexity.

00:12:44.200 --> 00:12:45.880
Which seems like
the usual tradeoff

00:12:45.880 --> 00:12:47.022
in pretty much everything.

00:12:47.022 --> 00:12:47.730
JAY SMITH: Right.

00:12:47.730 --> 00:12:52.520
Now being able to
essentially query the stream.

00:12:52.520 --> 00:12:54.820
So as its writing
to the logs, you're

00:12:54.820 --> 00:12:57.520
able to get information
not actually just waiting

00:12:57.520 --> 00:12:59.220
until it actually is written.

00:12:59.220 --> 00:13:01.720
GWEN SHAPIRA: And I'll show you
that on screen in real time.

00:13:01.720 --> 00:13:04.180
Just type a query and
see the events come in.

00:13:04.180 --> 00:13:07.740
It's pretty awesome.

00:13:07.740 --> 00:13:09.760
And this is like six
lines of code, or so.

00:13:09.760 --> 00:13:12.450
And it's six lines
because we broke it up.

00:13:12.450 --> 00:13:15.040
I wrote the same thing in Java.

00:13:15.040 --> 00:13:17.380
And it wasn't huge
amounts of code.

00:13:17.380 --> 00:13:21.310
But it was definitely three
screenfuls of lines written.

00:13:21.310 --> 00:13:23.860
So having the ability
to really do it quickly

00:13:23.860 --> 00:13:28.400
is pretty big for
busy developers.

00:13:28.400 --> 00:13:29.960
So what can we do with this?

00:13:29.960 --> 00:13:32.860
Our listeners at home
may want to go and write

00:13:32.860 --> 00:13:34.720
their own stream
processing framework.

00:13:34.720 --> 00:13:36.790
What could they do?

00:13:36.790 --> 00:13:41.950
So one thing that we see very
often is how companies have

00:13:41.950 --> 00:13:44.710
those clicks of events that are
actually meaningful information

00:13:44.710 --> 00:13:48.310
that they want to analyze--
sometimes from web applications

00:13:48.310 --> 00:13:49.490
or mobile apps--

00:13:49.490 --> 00:13:52.189
and then they may want to
enrich the event with things

00:13:52.189 --> 00:13:53.480
that they know about the users.

00:13:53.480 --> 00:13:56.530
So some profit that comes
from a database query.

00:13:56.530 --> 00:13:58.190
Maybe even log files
to see if there

00:13:58.190 --> 00:14:00.790
was correlation with different
errors that happened.

00:14:00.790 --> 00:14:02.650
And you can really
bring all of those

00:14:02.650 --> 00:14:05.030
into Kafka, probably
into different topics.

00:14:05.030 --> 00:14:08.140
Use KSQL to join,
window, aggregate,

00:14:08.140 --> 00:14:09.910
do whatever you want.

00:14:09.910 --> 00:14:11.800
And then you can really
stream the events

00:14:11.800 --> 00:14:14.260
into external systems
like Google BigQuery.

00:14:14.260 --> 00:14:16.280
So you can do more
analytical work.

00:14:16.280 --> 00:14:17.790
And that's one
thing to emphasize.

00:14:17.790 --> 00:14:20.800
KSQL is fantastic for
kind of data-managing,

00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:22.420
joining, grouping.

00:14:22.420 --> 00:14:24.850
And it works real
nicely in real time.

00:14:24.850 --> 00:14:27.520
But it's not like please
query the last five

00:14:27.520 --> 00:14:30.320
years of data kind of thing.

00:14:30.320 --> 00:14:32.920
And there is a lot of
stuff that really you

00:14:32.920 --> 00:14:34.780
want to write the data
to an external system

00:14:34.780 --> 00:14:37.702
so you can do a lot more
involved analytical work.

00:14:37.702 --> 00:14:38.410
JAY SMITH: Right.

00:14:38.410 --> 00:14:42.090
So you wouldn't necessarily
try to train a model with KSQL.

00:14:42.090 --> 00:14:45.520
Just more like getting
the information that you

00:14:45.520 --> 00:14:46.670
need to train the model.

00:14:46.670 --> 00:14:48.795
GWEN SHAPIRA: And you know
before you find a model,

00:14:48.795 --> 00:14:50.740
you really need clean,
high-quality data.

00:14:50.740 --> 00:14:53.485
This is where we see the
connectors and the KSQL

00:14:53.485 --> 00:14:54.110
really come in.

00:14:56.640 --> 00:14:59.110
But the big question is,
how do we actually do it?

00:14:59.110 --> 00:15:01.572
How do we get data
from Kafka to BigQuery?

00:15:01.572 --> 00:15:03.780
And it's one of the things
that I had my own opinion.

00:15:03.780 --> 00:15:04.941
And I said it's obvious.

00:15:04.941 --> 00:15:06.190
And then I asked other people.

00:15:06.190 --> 00:15:09.245
And they had their opinion
and they said it's obvious.

00:15:09.245 --> 00:15:11.730
So I want to go
through some options.

00:15:11.730 --> 00:15:15.420
So the old school way is
through something called Secor,

00:15:15.420 --> 00:15:17.580
which is basically a batch job.

00:15:17.580 --> 00:15:18.930
Highly parallelized back job.

00:15:18.930 --> 00:15:21.360
So think of it as a
big mass-produced job.

00:15:21.360 --> 00:15:24.150
And it basically goes,
connects to Kafka,

00:15:24.150 --> 00:15:27.662
starts consuming events,
and writes them to BigQuery.

00:15:27.662 --> 00:15:28.620
But this is a batch-up.

00:15:28.620 --> 00:15:31.110
So it's run once an hour or so.

00:15:31.110 --> 00:15:34.740
And while it's pretty cool,
and it's flexible, and does

00:15:34.740 --> 00:15:36.360
all kind of cool things--

00:15:36.360 --> 00:15:38.040
We did a lot of
work to make sure we

00:15:38.040 --> 00:15:39.180
get the data in real time.

00:15:39.180 --> 00:15:41.370
We don't want to lose
the real-time aspect just

00:15:41.370 --> 00:15:43.290
before we land in BigQuery.

00:15:43.290 --> 00:15:46.710
So we also picked up a
bunch of streaming options.

00:15:46.710 --> 00:15:49.085
And you can use stream
processing frameworks.

00:15:49.085 --> 00:15:50.210
And they give us examples--

00:15:50.210 --> 00:15:54.300
Kafka Streams, and Apache Beam,
and obviously Google Dataflow.

00:15:54.300 --> 00:15:58.320
And being full-fledged
programming languages,

00:15:58.320 --> 00:16:01.140
it gives you a lot of
control over what your data

00:16:01.140 --> 00:16:02.350
is going to look like.

00:16:02.350 --> 00:16:04.990
So if you need to do a
lot of standardization,

00:16:04.990 --> 00:16:07.770
a lot of clean-up, get
some data over here,

00:16:07.770 --> 00:16:10.110
some data over there,
a bunch of filtering--

00:16:10.110 --> 00:16:13.770
You can really write those
jobs and basically get events,

00:16:13.770 --> 00:16:16.230
clean them up, write
them to BigQuery.

00:16:16.230 --> 00:16:17.940
Of course, it's programming.

00:16:17.940 --> 00:16:19.680
So you have to
actually write all

00:16:19.680 --> 00:16:22.980
of it yourself-- all
the error-handling, all

00:16:22.980 --> 00:16:24.090
the scheme-handling.

00:16:24.090 --> 00:16:26.340
All those is your own code.

00:16:26.340 --> 00:16:29.430
And Kafka Connect is pretty
much the polar opposite.

00:16:29.430 --> 00:16:30.780
It's still streaming.

00:16:30.780 --> 00:16:33.767
But it's basically
a no-code option.

00:16:33.767 --> 00:16:34.350
I'll show you.

00:16:34.350 --> 00:16:37.570
Basically, write a big
configuration file.

00:16:37.570 --> 00:16:39.840
And you say, OK,
I'm getting data

00:16:39.840 --> 00:16:44.970
from this topic to this BigQuery
data set into that table.

00:16:44.970 --> 00:16:48.990
And you expect Kafka Connect
to handle everything for you,

00:16:48.990 --> 00:16:50.320
more or less.

00:16:50.320 --> 00:16:52.230
It's kind of like,
if this does the job,

00:16:52.230 --> 00:16:54.900
you're done in five
minutes, while writing code

00:16:54.900 --> 00:16:57.670
can take a big testing cycle.

00:16:57.670 --> 00:17:00.120
And if you actually
need the controls, and--

00:17:00.120 --> 00:17:02.760
I mean, it would be nice if I
could do it in five minutes.

00:17:02.760 --> 00:17:04.455
But I can't.

00:17:04.455 --> 00:17:06.329
JAY SMITH: So it's great
for getting started.

00:17:06.329 --> 00:17:08.287
GWEN SHAPIRA: It's amazing
for getting started.

00:17:08.287 --> 00:17:10.619
I'll show you how quickly
you can get it done.

00:17:13.619 --> 00:17:16.260
I'm thinking that
these days engineering

00:17:16.260 --> 00:17:18.690
is under a lot of
stress to deliver things

00:17:18.690 --> 00:17:21.060
in very short time cycle.

00:17:21.060 --> 00:17:24.000
And a lot of times, you
have to show results

00:17:24.000 --> 00:17:28.170
before you get allocated the
time to do anything smarter.

00:17:28.170 --> 00:17:32.700
So, like, look at I'm getting
events from Kafka to BigQuery.

00:17:32.700 --> 00:17:33.390
Fantastic.

00:17:33.390 --> 00:17:36.220
Oh I really need the events in
a slightly different format.

00:17:36.220 --> 00:17:38.530
So let's bring it to
the next two weeks.

00:17:38.530 --> 00:17:41.870
I'm going to really try to do
something a bit more involved.

00:17:45.180 --> 00:17:48.480
And then, once we know how
to collect a lot of events

00:17:48.480 --> 00:17:55.200
into Kafka and writes them into
BigQuery, why stop with clicks?

00:17:55.200 --> 00:17:58.360
I mean, there is a lot of
useful data in those legacy data

00:17:58.360 --> 00:18:00.610
systems and in
their applications.

00:18:00.610 --> 00:18:04.210
And you can use it for
example, maybe running a bank

00:18:04.210 --> 00:18:06.690
and you have your
credit card processing.

00:18:06.690 --> 00:18:08.970
You have transactions, and
you have loan requests.

00:18:08.970 --> 00:18:10.530
And you have a lot
of old information

00:18:10.530 --> 00:18:12.690
about the customer's
legacy systems.

00:18:12.690 --> 00:18:16.140
Bringing them all together and
processing them, getting them

00:18:16.140 --> 00:18:20.520
to Google BigQuery with
things like Connect or Google

00:18:20.520 --> 00:18:22.990
Dataflow, depending
on the use case.

00:18:22.990 --> 00:18:28.200
And then you really have a
lot of data here to process.

00:18:28.200 --> 00:18:30.480
Imagine trying to get access
to the data on the legacy

00:18:30.480 --> 00:18:30.980
mainframe.

00:18:30.980 --> 00:18:33.120
And the tools you would
have to use to do it.

00:18:33.120 --> 00:18:35.430
And then just how
long the processes

00:18:35.430 --> 00:18:37.785
take versus doing the
experimentation in data

00:18:37.785 --> 00:18:39.540
as it shows up in real time.

00:18:39.540 --> 00:18:41.850
It's a very big difference
in quality of life.

00:18:44.490 --> 00:18:47.900
And then if you got the
simple use cases working,

00:18:47.900 --> 00:18:51.050
you can do things that are more
advanced and obviously more

00:18:51.050 --> 00:18:51.780
exciting.

00:18:51.780 --> 00:18:53.060
I think it's more exciting.

00:18:53.060 --> 00:18:56.120
A lot of the data business
comes from devices.

00:18:56.120 --> 00:18:57.470
JAY SMITH: Yes it does.

00:18:57.470 --> 00:19:02.380
The IoT has made my life
easier, maybe a tad lazier.

00:19:02.380 --> 00:19:04.670
All of my outlets are wired.

00:19:04.670 --> 00:19:08.060
And I just speak into my Google
Home and turn on everything,

00:19:08.060 --> 00:19:09.170
turn off everything.

00:19:09.170 --> 00:19:10.160
GWEN SHAPIRA: You
actually do that?

00:19:10.160 --> 00:19:10.550
JAY SMITH: Yeah.

00:19:10.550 --> 00:19:11.925
GWEN SHAPIRA:
That's pretty cool.

00:19:11.925 --> 00:19:12.920
I'm not that advanced.

00:19:12.920 --> 00:19:17.660
But I do have things like Nest
and those programmable light

00:19:17.660 --> 00:19:18.300
bulbs.

00:19:18.300 --> 00:19:21.260
If you want to set up
a modem in my house.

00:19:21.260 --> 00:19:22.920
And it's a lot of fun.

00:19:22.920 --> 00:19:24.820
So basically all
of those devices

00:19:24.820 --> 00:19:27.420
speak a very small
number of protocols.

00:19:27.420 --> 00:19:29.320
A common one is MQTT.

00:19:29.320 --> 00:19:33.350
And part of that the Confluent
platform is an MQTT proxy.

00:19:33.350 --> 00:19:36.530
So you can really collect
information from those devices,

00:19:36.530 --> 00:19:37.616
stream it into the cloud.

00:19:37.616 --> 00:19:39.615
The nice thing about
clouds is, there's probably

00:19:39.615 --> 00:19:42.420
a cloud center near
a region nearby

00:19:42.420 --> 00:19:44.390
from where the device is.

00:19:44.390 --> 00:19:47.150
And then you can really
start doing advanced things.

00:19:47.150 --> 00:19:48.680
Like, you can train a model.

00:19:48.680 --> 00:19:51.800
And using the same
data, you can also

00:19:51.800 --> 00:19:53.330
serve the model in real time.

00:19:53.330 --> 00:19:55.980
Something like Google
Function serving up the model

00:19:55.980 --> 00:19:58.040
on every event as it happens.

00:19:58.040 --> 00:20:01.440
So you can really start with
something fairly pedestrian

00:20:01.440 --> 00:20:03.720
like clickstream
processing and end up

00:20:03.720 --> 00:20:05.780
with a real time
machine learning

00:20:05.780 --> 00:20:09.377
IoT system that is
probably something that you

00:20:09.377 --> 00:20:10.460
can raise some money with.

00:20:15.230 --> 00:20:18.255
So obviously with all that,
you want to actually use

00:20:18.255 --> 00:20:19.130
all those cool tools.

00:20:19.130 --> 00:20:21.170
You don't necessarily
want to install them,

00:20:21.170 --> 00:20:24.019
configures them, figure out
why they are losing data.

00:20:24.019 --> 00:20:25.685
Or maybe they're not
losing data and why

00:20:25.685 --> 00:20:27.180
they are not losing data.

00:20:27.180 --> 00:20:29.210
Why they are not losing
data, but doing it

00:20:29.210 --> 00:20:31.040
too slow, or too fast.

00:20:31.040 --> 00:20:33.040
Managing it, upgrading
it, troubleshooting it,

00:20:33.040 --> 00:20:35.120
getting the pager at 3 AM.

00:20:35.120 --> 00:20:38.450
Have you ever had a pager job?

00:20:38.450 --> 00:20:39.660
JAY SMITH: Not for a while.

00:20:39.660 --> 00:20:41.600
But I do remember
getting those alerts

00:20:41.600 --> 00:20:42.260
in the middle of the night.

00:20:42.260 --> 00:20:43.843
GWEN SHAPIRA: Yeah,
I'm just thinking,

00:20:43.843 --> 00:20:45.770
my last pager job
was maybe in 2009.

00:20:45.770 --> 00:20:48.260
I do not miss that at all.

00:20:48.260 --> 00:20:50.839
So basically introducing the--

00:20:50.839 --> 00:20:52.880
or not introducing, it's
been around for a while.

00:20:52.880 --> 00:20:56.080
But the idea is that if
you could write yourself,

00:20:56.080 --> 00:20:57.050
Kafka is open source.

00:20:57.050 --> 00:20:58.520
You're can install and run it.

00:20:58.520 --> 00:21:02.090
Or you can let us run it for
you on the Google platform.

00:21:02.090 --> 00:21:03.100
And I'll show you.

00:21:03.100 --> 00:21:05.225
It's kind of integrated
with the rest of the Google

00:21:05.225 --> 00:21:06.820
platform with those connectors.

00:21:06.820 --> 00:21:10.180
JAY SMITH: Yeah, one thing I
always tell users, developers.

00:21:10.180 --> 00:21:12.180
You know, when you're
building your application,

00:21:12.180 --> 00:21:17.730
if it's not something that
is core to your project--

00:21:17.730 --> 00:21:20.450
If it's something that
you need but managing it

00:21:20.450 --> 00:21:22.190
doesn't really add value--

00:21:22.190 --> 00:21:24.470
it's always best to have
somebody else manage it.

00:21:24.470 --> 00:21:27.610
That way you can focus all
your time, resources, energies,

00:21:27.610 --> 00:21:31.390
onto the things that actually
matter for your application.

00:21:31.390 --> 00:21:32.480
And for your business.

00:21:32.480 --> 00:21:34.670
GWEN SHAPIRA: And the
really funny thing

00:21:34.670 --> 00:21:37.040
is that I've been working
with the same companies

00:21:37.040 --> 00:21:38.600
for a bunch of years now.

00:21:38.600 --> 00:21:40.100
And it's funny
how companies that

00:21:40.100 --> 00:21:43.950
three years ago were like, we
are never going to use cloud.

00:21:43.950 --> 00:21:47.599
Clouds will not work
for our banking system.

00:21:47.599 --> 00:21:49.140
And then three years
later, are like,

00:21:49.140 --> 00:21:51.580
oh you know we are actually
starting to move to the cloud.

00:21:51.580 --> 00:21:53.170
We could use some help.

00:21:53.170 --> 00:21:56.210
What's a good architecture
for migrating to the cloud?

00:21:56.210 --> 00:21:57.260
What's a good use case?

00:21:57.260 --> 00:21:59.127
How do you do cloud security?

00:21:59.127 --> 00:22:00.960
And I think it's in
just the economics of it

00:22:00.960 --> 00:22:02.050
are kind of individual.

00:22:02.050 --> 00:22:04.500
Why would you want to run
your own data center it's not

00:22:04.500 --> 00:22:05.220
your competency?

00:22:05.220 --> 00:22:06.170
JAY SMITH: Absolutely.

00:22:06.170 --> 00:22:09.050
It just doesn't
make sense to do it

00:22:09.050 --> 00:22:11.210
if it doesn't benefit
your application

00:22:11.210 --> 00:22:12.310
at the end of the day.

00:22:12.310 --> 00:22:12.720
GWEN SHAPIRA: Exactly.

00:22:12.720 --> 00:22:14.620
Focus on things you
are actually good at.

00:22:14.620 --> 00:22:16.992
In banking case,
taking our money.

00:22:16.992 --> 00:22:18.950
JAY SMITH: Let's look at
Confluent Cloud, then.

00:22:18.950 --> 00:22:19.940
GWEN SHAPIRA: Yes.

00:22:19.940 --> 00:22:23.050
Let me try to switch to my demo.

00:22:23.050 --> 00:22:25.880
So I'm already logged
into the Confluent Cloud.

00:22:25.880 --> 00:22:29.030
And you can see that I basically
have a bunch of clusters

00:22:29.030 --> 00:22:30.290
already here.

00:22:30.290 --> 00:22:33.510
If I want to add a new one,
it's really not a big deal.

00:22:33.510 --> 00:22:35.960
So let's say I want
to test something new.

00:22:35.960 --> 00:22:38.230
And you can see I can play
around with how much data

00:22:38.230 --> 00:22:40.430
I am time consuming
and producing.

00:22:40.430 --> 00:22:44.360
Important bit is to select the
right provider, right region,

00:22:44.360 --> 00:22:45.980
how many availability zones.

00:22:45.980 --> 00:22:48.380
And then we click continue.

00:22:48.380 --> 00:22:50.670
And look, this is
going to cost us,

00:22:50.670 --> 00:22:52.850
say, half a dollar per hour.

00:22:52.850 --> 00:22:54.050
And we're here for an hour.

00:22:54.050 --> 00:22:56.445
So I think you better pay up.

00:22:56.445 --> 00:22:57.320
JAY SMITH: All right.

00:22:57.320 --> 00:22:58.819
I'll send it to you
over Google Pay.

00:23:01.452 --> 00:23:02.160
GWEN SHAPIRA: OK.

00:23:02.160 --> 00:23:04.080
So after I launch the
cluster, I basically

00:23:04.080 --> 00:23:08.610
see a bunch of instructions on
how to install a C Cloud, which

00:23:08.610 --> 00:23:11.850
is inspired by G Cloud--

00:23:11.850 --> 00:23:13.290
which we all know and love.

00:23:13.290 --> 00:23:18.210
And this allows me to
basically use the cloud

00:23:18.210 --> 00:23:19.320
from the command line.

00:23:23.830 --> 00:23:31.020
Basically I can do something
like C Cloud topics list.

00:23:31.020 --> 00:23:32.680
And because I'm
already connected

00:23:32.680 --> 00:23:34.120
to one of my
clusters, you can see

00:23:34.120 --> 00:23:36.940
I have a pretty big list
of topics going on here.

00:23:36.940 --> 00:23:38.570
I wish I sorted them.

00:23:38.570 --> 00:23:40.900
It's kind of not really sorted.

00:23:40.900 --> 00:23:42.830
I can create a new topic.

00:23:42.830 --> 00:23:44.790
That's going to be
kind of a new topic.

00:23:44.790 --> 00:23:47.370
And I can produce events
into a topic if I want to.

00:23:47.370 --> 00:23:49.370
So you want me to show
you how to produce events

00:23:49.370 --> 00:23:50.395
in to a topic?

00:23:50.395 --> 00:23:51.686
JAY SMITH: Sure, let's do that.

00:23:57.847 --> 00:23:59.930
GWEN SHAPIRA: So this was
the new topic I created.

00:24:25.610 --> 00:24:29.020
OK, so I produced an
event, super-simple.

00:24:29.020 --> 00:24:30.860
And now I want to
obviously consume it.

00:24:36.047 --> 00:24:37.880
And I'm doing [INAUDIBLE]
to start consuming

00:24:37.880 --> 00:24:38.700
from the beginning.

00:24:38.700 --> 00:24:40.130
Otherwise, you only
get new events.

00:24:40.130 --> 00:24:42.380
And you'll see that they are
starting for a long time.

00:24:42.380 --> 00:24:44.550
Why is nothing showing up?

00:24:44.550 --> 00:24:46.340
So this does take a
bit of time because we

00:24:46.340 --> 00:24:48.190
need to negotiate
the consumer group.

00:24:48.190 --> 00:24:50.490
And see if someone
else's connects.

00:24:50.490 --> 00:24:53.340
But soon enough, here is a load.

00:24:53.340 --> 00:24:55.370
So hey, if you're
following at home,

00:24:55.370 --> 00:24:58.730
this may be the first
event you and consume.

00:24:58.730 --> 00:25:00.830
I know for me, when
I teach classes,

00:25:00.830 --> 00:25:03.420
it's always exciting to see
people produce and consume

00:25:03.420 --> 00:25:04.730
their first event.

00:25:04.730 --> 00:25:06.470
But what I really
wanted to show you

00:25:06.470 --> 00:25:07.760
is how you build a pipeline.

00:25:07.760 --> 00:25:11.210
So how I get events
from Wikipedia.

00:25:11.210 --> 00:25:14.150
And maybe run a KSQL query.

00:25:14.150 --> 00:25:16.700
And then write them to BigQuery.

00:25:16.700 --> 00:25:21.080
You can show me how to
do a query on BigQuery.

00:25:21.080 --> 00:25:23.660
So I'm switching to
Confluent control center.

00:25:26.750 --> 00:25:29.080
And basically, I can
see all the topics here.

00:25:29.080 --> 00:25:31.240
But I really wanted to
show you the connectors.

00:25:31.240 --> 00:25:33.580
So you can see that they
have a Wikipedia connector.

00:25:33.580 --> 00:25:35.330
And the only expense--
as connectors, they

00:25:35.330 --> 00:25:36.500
don't require to write code.

00:25:36.500 --> 00:25:38.110
You basically configure them.

00:25:38.110 --> 00:25:39.010
So over here.

00:25:39.010 --> 00:25:41.020
I basically configured
the Wikipedia server.

00:25:41.020 --> 00:25:41.940
It's an RC server.

00:25:41.940 --> 00:25:43.750
Wikipedia publishes
everything that

00:25:43.750 --> 00:25:46.780
happens to a bunch of channels.

00:25:46.780 --> 00:25:49.447
And you can see all the
channels that I'm reading from.

00:25:49.447 --> 00:25:51.280
It's basically a channel
for every language.

00:25:51.280 --> 00:25:55.970
And I'm writing all of them
to the same Wikipedia topic.

00:25:55.970 --> 00:25:59.440
So let me start that.

00:25:59.440 --> 00:26:01.730
And then let's go
and look at the data.

00:26:01.730 --> 00:26:06.161
So I'm looking for
the Wikipedia topic.

00:26:06.161 --> 00:26:08.660
When I click here, you can see
that I have a bunch of screen

00:26:08.660 --> 00:26:10.850
so I can see the
schema in the topic.

00:26:10.850 --> 00:26:12.030
And I can take a look.

00:26:12.030 --> 00:26:13.650
You can see this is
real-time events.

00:26:13.650 --> 00:26:15.380
You can see more
events are coming in.

00:26:15.380 --> 00:26:17.090
And I can see the new events.

00:26:17.090 --> 00:26:19.740
And so you can
actually see the data.

00:26:19.740 --> 00:26:21.830
So we're combing Wikipedia.

00:26:21.830 --> 00:26:23.870
And this user edited this page.

00:26:23.870 --> 00:26:25.670
He put in this message.

00:26:25.670 --> 00:26:27.890
I find it pretty cool.

00:26:27.890 --> 00:26:32.120
Now if you want to actually
run a query, I can go--

00:26:32.120 --> 00:26:33.770
Oh, it's unable to connect.

00:26:33.770 --> 00:26:35.510
I cannot run queries for you.

00:26:35.510 --> 00:26:36.470
I'm sorry.

00:26:36.470 --> 00:26:37.860
JAY SMITH: It's all right.

00:26:37.860 --> 00:26:39.419
Let's see what else
we can do here.

00:26:39.419 --> 00:26:40.210
GWEN SHAPIRA: Yeah.

00:26:40.210 --> 00:26:41.584
That's what happens
on the things

00:26:41.584 --> 00:26:43.570
that you didn't test
the night before.

00:26:43.570 --> 00:26:46.390
Pretty much, that's
how demos work.

00:26:46.390 --> 00:26:49.880
But I can go to the
sync and basically

00:26:49.880 --> 00:26:51.950
say, so Wikipedia
edits will not exist,

00:26:51.950 --> 00:26:53.640
because that's the
one with the things

00:26:53.640 --> 00:26:56.880
that we were supposed
to get processed.

00:26:56.880 --> 00:27:01.730
But we can still get Wikipedia
events into BigQuery.

00:27:01.730 --> 00:27:03.420
So we edit here.

00:27:03.420 --> 00:27:07.060
And here, you can see
that I'm getting events

00:27:07.060 --> 00:27:08.980
from those two topics.

00:27:08.980 --> 00:27:12.370
And I'm basically mapping them.

00:27:12.370 --> 00:27:13.420
Go to Wikipedia edits.

00:27:13.420 --> 00:27:14.700
Wikipedia goes to Wikipedia.

00:27:14.700 --> 00:27:19.050
So that's basically a topic
to table mapping for BigQuery.

00:27:19.050 --> 00:27:21.340
And you can say, this
is my BigQuery project.

00:27:21.340 --> 00:27:24.070
And the name of the
data set is Wikipedia.

00:27:24.070 --> 00:27:25.870
It took me a long
time to figure out

00:27:25.870 --> 00:27:27.820
how all this terminology works.

00:27:27.820 --> 00:27:30.820
But it's the same terminology
if you know BigQuery.

00:27:30.820 --> 00:27:33.430
It will probably be a lot easier
for you than it was for me.

00:27:33.430 --> 00:27:37.360
I figured out how to use the
JSON key file to actually

00:27:37.360 --> 00:27:40.630
authorize my BigQuery user.

00:27:40.630 --> 00:27:44.260
And so now after I
figure this all out,

00:27:44.260 --> 00:27:46.132
I can start my connector.

00:27:55.408 --> 00:27:57.350
I don't know how to switch tabs.

00:27:57.350 --> 00:27:59.300
So it's going to
be that hard way.

00:27:59.300 --> 00:28:03.910
So this is my Google Dashboard.

00:28:03.910 --> 00:28:05.780
And here I have BigQuery.

00:28:05.780 --> 00:28:07.440
This is the data set.

00:28:07.440 --> 00:28:10.240
And I have to create a data set.

00:28:10.240 --> 00:28:12.760
But before I start
the connector,

00:28:12.760 --> 00:28:14.320
I had to create a
Wikipedia data set.

00:28:14.320 --> 00:28:16.750
But the tables will basically
be automatically created

00:28:16.750 --> 00:28:18.010
based on the mapping.

00:28:18.010 --> 00:28:20.170
And their schema will
be automatically created

00:28:20.170 --> 00:28:22.360
based on the schema
of the events in Kafka

00:28:22.360 --> 00:28:23.764
that I showed you.

00:28:23.764 --> 00:28:25.680
And if I make a change
to the event in Kafka--

00:28:25.680 --> 00:28:29.710
if I could use KSQL to
actually edit the events--

00:28:29.710 --> 00:28:32.290
the change will automatically
reflect in BigQuery.

00:28:32.290 --> 00:28:34.060
So that's pretty cool.

00:28:34.060 --> 00:28:35.980
And it actually shows
me all my query history.

00:28:35.980 --> 00:28:37.600
I didn't know it does that.

00:28:37.600 --> 00:28:38.710
That's neat.

00:28:38.710 --> 00:28:41.380
So it can actually,
as well, do something

00:28:41.380 --> 00:28:46.070
like, you can see all of
those were Wikipedia edits.

00:28:46.070 --> 00:28:48.580
But I want to do
something from--

00:28:48.580 --> 00:28:51.235
does it do auto-complete?

00:28:51.235 --> 00:28:52.897
Had the source.

00:28:52.897 --> 00:28:54.230
Can I talk to a product manager?

00:28:54.230 --> 00:28:55.410
I want auto-complete.

00:28:55.410 --> 00:28:57.320
JAY SMITH: We'll
put it on the list.

00:28:57.320 --> 00:28:59.940
GWEN SHAPIRA: Then let's get
just like a few of those.

00:29:04.548 --> 00:29:05.790
OK, let me see.

00:29:05.790 --> 00:29:09.316
If I click here, it will
just run the query for me?

00:29:09.316 --> 00:29:13.300
Query table, yeah.

00:29:13.300 --> 00:29:16.492
Oh I needed to have the
fully-qualified table now.

00:29:24.410 --> 00:29:27.370
Hopefully, we don't have
results from today yet.

00:29:27.370 --> 00:29:28.620
JAY SMITH: No, it's still new.

00:29:28.620 --> 00:29:29.788
That's all right, though.

00:29:29.788 --> 00:29:31.746
GWEN SHAPIRA: Yeah that's
a risk of live demos.

00:29:31.746 --> 00:29:33.570
JAY SMITH: Looks
like all of them.

00:29:33.570 --> 00:29:34.530
We've all been there.

00:29:38.709 --> 00:29:41.250
GWEN SHAPIRA: You can see here
basically the Wikipedia pages.

00:29:41.250 --> 00:29:42.930
You can see the channels.

00:29:42.930 --> 00:29:45.780
I can also show you
since I think I still

00:29:45.780 --> 00:29:48.240
have all data from
the Wikipedia edits,

00:29:48.240 --> 00:29:51.600
I can probably show you how
I enriched it with KSQL.

00:29:51.600 --> 00:29:53.290
But when KSQL still
worked for me.

00:29:58.130 --> 00:30:00.550
JAY SMITH: You might want
to get rid of the partition.

00:30:03.251 --> 00:30:04.790
Give an asterisk too.

00:30:08.185 --> 00:30:10.310
GWEN SHAPIRA: I really like
how it shows the arrows

00:30:10.310 --> 00:30:12.940
that they have.

00:30:12.940 --> 00:30:14.740
That's super-helpful.

00:30:14.740 --> 00:30:17.570
And now it's going to work.

00:30:17.570 --> 00:30:19.720
See, it parses
everything in real time.

00:30:19.720 --> 00:30:21.440
Real time just adds
a lot more usability

00:30:21.440 --> 00:30:23.060
to pretty much everything.

00:30:23.060 --> 00:30:26.620
So you can see here is that
I added a readable date.

00:30:26.620 --> 00:30:30.670
So now you know that I did
all my practice five days ago.

00:30:30.670 --> 00:30:33.840
And if you go to the end, you
can see that I edited language.

00:30:33.840 --> 00:30:35.250
The way I edited
the language, is

00:30:35.250 --> 00:30:40.060
that it's based on channel
line was like fr dot Wikipedia.

00:30:40.060 --> 00:30:43.060
I basically joined
it with a table

00:30:43.060 --> 00:30:47.290
that I created that has mapping
of the channel to the language.

00:30:47.290 --> 00:30:51.171
And then I could
do a join and get--

00:30:51.171 --> 00:30:51.670
Yeah.

00:30:51.670 --> 00:30:53.870
That is pretty awesome.

00:30:53.870 --> 00:31:00.250
So basically, this allows us
to get events from Wikipedia

00:31:00.250 --> 00:31:01.510
to Kafka.

00:31:01.510 --> 00:31:04.600
If we are lucky it will do
some extreme processing.

00:31:04.600 --> 00:31:09.350
And then use Connect to
write the events to BigQuery.

00:31:09.350 --> 00:31:10.829
And all that in real time.

00:31:10.829 --> 00:31:13.120
JAY SMITH: And this is just
one of the many ways we can

00:31:13.120 --> 00:31:15.624
use Kafka and Confluent Cloud.

00:31:15.624 --> 00:31:16.540
GWEN SHAPIRA: Exactly.

00:31:16.540 --> 00:31:17.480
Yes.

00:31:17.480 --> 00:31:20.770
And the next time, we'll
try to demo TensorFlow.

00:31:20.770 --> 00:31:24.660
With a small IoT device that
kind of drives around here.

00:31:28.480 --> 00:31:32.930
Just in case people
want to try it.

00:31:32.930 --> 00:31:34.180
Here we are.

00:31:34.180 --> 00:31:38.450
And basically, we have a
Confluent Cloud professional,

00:31:38.450 --> 00:31:40.750
which is exactly the
OIs that I showed you.

00:31:40.750 --> 00:31:46.680
So you can just, you write
Confluent.cloud or you

00:31:46.680 --> 00:31:48.010
go to that URL.

00:31:48.010 --> 00:31:50.650
And you basically
sign up and you say,

00:31:50.650 --> 00:31:52.180
it's like half a
dollar per hour.

00:31:52.180 --> 00:31:57.280
And building this pipeline takes
just I don't know, an hour.

00:31:57.280 --> 00:32:00.370
And it's basically
something that you can

00:32:00.370 --> 00:32:03.275
get started with fairly easily.

00:32:03.275 --> 00:32:04.150
JAY SMITH: All right.

00:32:04.150 --> 00:32:06.340
Well that was great.

00:32:06.340 --> 00:32:09.160
Stay tuned for a live
Q&amp;A. We will come back

00:32:09.160 --> 00:32:10.210
in about a minute.

00:32:10.210 --> 00:32:12.840
And we will answer your
questions about Confluent Cloud

00:32:12.840 --> 00:32:13.340
and Kafka.

00:33:26.260 --> 00:33:26.920
Welcome back.

00:33:26.920 --> 00:33:29.110
Looks like we've got some
really good questions.

00:33:29.110 --> 00:33:31.720
So why don't we jump
right into them?

00:33:31.720 --> 00:33:34.180
How secure is data
in Confluent Cloud?

00:33:34.180 --> 00:33:36.790
What measures do you have
in place to protect data?

00:33:36.790 --> 00:33:39.248
GWEN SHAPIRA: Isn't it the
first thing that everyone always

00:33:39.248 --> 00:33:40.190
asks about the cloud?

00:33:40.190 --> 00:33:42.600
How do you keep our data safe?

00:33:42.600 --> 00:33:46.390
So Kafka itself has
basically the authorization,

00:33:46.390 --> 00:33:49.630
authentication, encryption-- the
things you need to keep safe.

00:33:49.630 --> 00:33:53.650
In Confluent Cloud by
default, all the communication

00:33:53.650 --> 00:33:55.090
is SSL encrypted.

00:33:55.090 --> 00:33:57.040
So you have encryption
in the wire.

00:33:57.040 --> 00:33:59.800
We use encrypted storage
to store the data.

00:33:59.800 --> 00:34:01.850
So you have encryption on disk.

00:34:01.850 --> 00:34:04.060
And you authorize
which is basically,

00:34:04.060 --> 00:34:06.820
you get an API key and a secret.

00:34:06.820 --> 00:34:08.659
As long as you keep them safe.

00:34:08.659 --> 00:34:10.380
we don't even know them.

00:34:10.380 --> 00:34:15.489
And basically, that's the
authentication method.

00:34:15.489 --> 00:34:21.400
If you are even more
paranoid than that.

00:34:21.400 --> 00:34:24.639
I mean in this case, I
exposed my Kafka to the world.

00:34:24.639 --> 00:34:26.409
Like I could just
use it from my laptop

00:34:26.409 --> 00:34:30.080
from the Google
Wi-Fi with no issues.

00:34:30.080 --> 00:34:33.880
But if you want to
limit the access

00:34:33.880 --> 00:34:36.260
to just who's in
your own data center,

00:34:36.260 --> 00:34:38.440
and just within
your company VPN,

00:34:38.440 --> 00:34:40.489
then we also
support VPC peering.

00:34:40.489 --> 00:34:44.840
So you can use this to have an
even more secure experience.

00:34:44.840 --> 00:34:48.730
To be honest, I'm exposing my
toy clusters to the internet.

00:34:48.730 --> 00:34:50.949
But I wouldn't probably
do it if it was actually

00:34:50.949 --> 00:34:51.908
running my bank.

00:34:51.908 --> 00:34:53.949
JAY SMITH: And then being
hosted on Google Cloud,

00:34:53.949 --> 00:34:57.400
you guys get to have the
added benefit of the security

00:34:57.400 --> 00:34:58.650
that Google Cloud offers.

00:34:58.650 --> 00:34:58.990
GWEN SHAPIRA: Yes.

00:34:58.990 --> 00:35:00.940
For example, your load
balancer is probably

00:35:00.940 --> 00:35:04.574
what keeps us safe
from DDoS attacks.

00:35:04.574 --> 00:35:06.490
JAY SMITH: We currently
have some applications

00:35:06.490 --> 00:35:08.230
running in our data center.

00:35:08.230 --> 00:35:10.690
But we would also like to
use BigQuery in real-time

00:35:10.690 --> 00:35:14.410
with data generated by our
on-premises applications.

00:35:14.410 --> 00:35:16.171
Is this possible with Kafka?

00:35:16.171 --> 00:35:18.420
GWEN SHAPIRA: I think I just
showed that it is, right?

00:35:18.420 --> 00:35:21.520
I mean, you don't have to
get data from Wikipedia.

00:35:21.520 --> 00:35:24.550
It was a cute demo,
but then you can really

00:35:24.550 --> 00:35:27.100
get the data from
anything that you produce

00:35:27.100 --> 00:35:29.130
to Kafka on Google Cloud.

00:35:29.130 --> 00:35:31.600
You can use Connect and
the BigQuery connector

00:35:31.600 --> 00:35:34.930
to get the data to
Google BigQuery.

00:35:34.930 --> 00:35:37.240
If you don't want to use
the BigQuery connector,

00:35:37.240 --> 00:35:42.280
you can also use Data Flow,
or Kafka Streams, or something

00:35:42.280 --> 00:35:45.280
[? better, ?] or code that you
wrote yourself, because you

00:35:45.280 --> 00:35:47.140
are not a invented here person.

00:35:47.140 --> 00:35:48.005
The sky's the limit.

00:35:48.005 --> 00:35:48.880
JAY SMITH: All right.

00:35:48.880 --> 00:35:52.300
With that REST API, you
were able to write a lot.

00:35:52.300 --> 00:35:54.490
We have implemented
Kafka ourselves

00:35:54.490 --> 00:35:57.180
and have been running
it for three years now.

00:35:57.180 --> 00:36:01.310
As Kafka adoption increased, we
are experiencing some issues.

00:36:01.310 --> 00:36:03.260
It is lagging quite a bit.

00:36:03.260 --> 00:36:04.930
And this is impacting
our business.

00:36:04.930 --> 00:36:07.450
Do you have any recommendations
on what we should look into?

00:36:07.450 --> 00:36:09.874
GWEN SHAPIRA: Oh,
tuning is a big topic.

00:36:09.874 --> 00:36:11.290
So I'm going to
assume that you're

00:36:11.290 --> 00:36:12.910
running on Confluent Cloud.

00:36:12.910 --> 00:36:16.090
And obviously, the [INAUDIBLE]
are running perfectly well.

00:36:16.090 --> 00:36:20.200
So it's really more about
looking into your consumers--

00:36:20.200 --> 00:36:22.210
like your application,
why is it lagging?

00:36:22.210 --> 00:36:24.610
So first of all, kudos for
noticing that your application

00:36:24.610 --> 00:36:25.390
is lagging.

00:36:25.390 --> 00:36:28.630
We've seen people who do
not monitor consumer lag,

00:36:28.630 --> 00:36:31.630
and therefore don't even know
that their application is

00:36:31.630 --> 00:36:32.350
lagging.

00:36:32.350 --> 00:36:34.700
So if you are monitoring it
and you know it's lagging,

00:36:34.700 --> 00:36:38.120
you're already a step
ahead of everyone else.

00:36:38.120 --> 00:36:41.909
And then, most
lag-monitoring tools--

00:36:41.909 --> 00:36:44.200
definitely in Control Center,
but I think also the rest

00:36:44.200 --> 00:36:44.850
of them--

00:36:44.850 --> 00:36:48.710
is they will basically show
you what the lag looks like.

00:36:48.710 --> 00:36:51.080
Is it increasing or decreasing?

00:36:51.080 --> 00:36:53.080
You fell behind and
you cannot catch up?

00:36:53.080 --> 00:36:56.110
It will also show you how
many partitions you have,

00:36:56.110 --> 00:36:59.440
how many consumers you have,
and the lag on each partition.

00:36:59.440 --> 00:37:02.020
So you can say, oh,
actually this consumer

00:37:02.020 --> 00:37:04.510
is reading from five
partitions, while the other two

00:37:04.510 --> 00:37:07.310
consumers are barely
reading from two partitions.

00:37:07.310 --> 00:37:10.990
And we need to somehow reassign
partitions and rebalance,

00:37:10.990 --> 00:37:13.850
because it's clear why
this one is falling behind.

00:37:13.850 --> 00:37:16.960
You can say, oh, it's one
consumer, 100 partitions,

00:37:16.960 --> 00:37:18.340
loss of data gets returned.

00:37:18.340 --> 00:37:22.660
It may have been OK 10
years ago, maybe not OK now.

00:37:22.660 --> 00:37:25.930
And then sometimes you also have
a problem where one partition

00:37:25.930 --> 00:37:27.400
just gets more data.

00:37:27.400 --> 00:37:30.270
And you need to consider the
keys that you're writing.

00:37:30.270 --> 00:37:34.240
How do you really distribute
the events to partitions

00:37:34.240 --> 00:37:36.640
to avoid this kind of
skew, because it can also

00:37:36.640 --> 00:37:38.260
cause you to lag.

00:37:38.260 --> 00:37:39.980
If you're running
Kafka yourself,

00:37:39.980 --> 00:37:42.130
obviously there is a lot
of issues that can happen.

00:37:42.130 --> 00:37:44.020
It can be that a
broker is handling

00:37:44.020 --> 00:37:45.350
a lot more logs than the rest.

00:37:45.350 --> 00:37:46.600
And you need to rebalance.

00:37:46.600 --> 00:37:49.511
It can be that you have a
bad disk for that matter.

00:37:49.511 --> 00:37:51.010
It can be a lot of
different things.

00:37:54.340 --> 00:37:56.800
JAY SMITH: That helps narrow
it down a little bit though.

00:37:56.800 --> 00:37:57.820
GWEN SHAPIRA: Just a tiny bit.

00:37:57.820 --> 00:38:00.320
That's the nice thing about not
running the broker yourself.

00:38:00.320 --> 00:38:01.870
At least you know
that this part--

00:38:01.870 --> 00:38:03.820
you figure it out who
has a call to support.

00:38:03.820 --> 00:38:07.247
And then you only have
to worry about the rest.

00:38:07.247 --> 00:38:08.830
JAY SMITH: We are
cloud-native company

00:38:08.830 --> 00:38:11.140
and are looking for
Kafka as a service.

00:38:11.140 --> 00:38:13.210
What are some things
we should consider when

00:38:13.210 --> 00:38:14.707
selecting a particular service?

00:38:14.707 --> 00:38:15.290
Good question.

00:38:15.290 --> 00:38:18.600
We have a lot of people nowadays
that are born in the cloud.

00:38:18.600 --> 00:38:21.730
Their applications never saw
an on-premise data center.

00:38:24.370 --> 00:38:26.710
GWEN SHAPIRA: For me, I'm
very much open-source person.

00:38:26.710 --> 00:38:30.400
I think you should
try to avoid look-in.

00:38:30.400 --> 00:38:32.300
And try to make sure
you're using open source

00:38:32.300 --> 00:38:34.540
software, open standards.

00:38:34.540 --> 00:38:37.900
And just look at the
community and make sure

00:38:37.900 --> 00:38:39.690
that you're working
with the communities

00:38:39.690 --> 00:38:41.495
that you enjoy working with.

00:38:41.495 --> 00:38:43.620
You obviously want to make
sure that the people who

00:38:43.620 --> 00:38:45.370
are running the
software are actually

00:38:45.370 --> 00:38:48.970
capable of supporting it.

00:38:48.970 --> 00:38:51.520
I guess it is, but this
is just due diligence.

00:38:55.560 --> 00:38:56.880
I'm trying to see.

00:38:56.880 --> 00:38:59.232
Do you have suggestions?

00:38:59.232 --> 00:39:01.190
JAY SMITH: Testimonials
is always a good thing.

00:39:01.190 --> 00:39:02.648
Whenever I am
looking for somebody,

00:39:02.648 --> 00:39:05.270
I try to find somebody
who else has used it.

00:39:05.270 --> 00:39:06.180
What did they use?

00:39:06.180 --> 00:39:08.190
How they felt about it.

00:39:08.190 --> 00:39:10.340
It's important to
emphasize though,

00:39:10.340 --> 00:39:14.720
that Confluent has a lot of the
original designers, creators

00:39:14.720 --> 00:39:15.660
of Kafka.

00:39:15.660 --> 00:39:17.365
So that's one way.

00:39:17.365 --> 00:39:19.240
That's a little bit of
a selling point there.

00:39:19.240 --> 00:39:21.900
GWEN SHAPIRA: And as I said,
is that just as a developer,

00:39:21.900 --> 00:39:24.020
you want to work
with nice tools.

00:39:24.020 --> 00:39:26.990
I think the reasons that
I enjoy Google Cloud

00:39:26.990 --> 00:39:29.420
and really was something we
tried to emulate in Confluent

00:39:29.420 --> 00:39:33.980
Cloud is really have
a nice command line,

00:39:33.980 --> 00:39:35.870
APIs that are usable.

00:39:35.870 --> 00:39:37.990
Just make sure that
it's a good-- you're

00:39:37.990 --> 00:39:40.460
going to spend a lot of
time in that environment

00:39:40.460 --> 00:39:41.660
if you're a developer.

00:39:41.660 --> 00:39:45.420
So it has to be a
nice environment.

00:39:45.420 --> 00:39:48.930
JAY SMITH: Final question, we
are heavily invested in GCP

00:39:48.930 --> 00:39:50.550
and are adopting Kafka.

00:39:50.550 --> 00:39:55.560
Is Confluent Kafka
the same as GCP Kafka?

00:39:55.560 --> 00:39:58.410
Is it a native GCP service?

00:39:58.410 --> 00:40:00.330
Well, I can answer the GCP part.

00:40:00.330 --> 00:40:04.530
Today we do not have
a managed Kafka.

00:40:04.530 --> 00:40:08.190
But we have a partnership
with Confluent

00:40:08.190 --> 00:40:10.767
to provide you
managed Kafka on GCP.

00:40:10.767 --> 00:40:12.600
GWEN SHAPIRA: So the
big difference I guess,

00:40:12.600 --> 00:40:15.510
is that if it's not
an active GCP service,

00:40:15.510 --> 00:40:18.780
you don't get to see it on
the big Google Dashboard where

00:40:18.780 --> 00:40:21.590
a so BigQuery, for example.

00:40:21.590 --> 00:40:23.550
But other than
that, we are running

00:40:23.550 --> 00:40:26.060
on the GCP infrastructure.

00:40:26.060 --> 00:40:28.200
I think specifically, on GKE.

00:40:28.200 --> 00:40:34.590
We're using all the
available Google tooling.

00:40:34.590 --> 00:40:36.240
It is not a GCP Kafka.

00:40:36.240 --> 00:40:40.600
But hopefully you kind
of get a GCP experience

00:40:40.600 --> 00:40:41.830
that you know and love.

00:40:41.830 --> 00:40:42.538
JAY SMITH: Right.

00:40:42.538 --> 00:40:45.060
And running Confluent
Cloud on GCP,

00:40:45.060 --> 00:40:47.410
you can integrate with
every other GCP tool

00:40:47.410 --> 00:40:49.040
like we saw BigQuery earlier.

00:40:49.040 --> 00:40:54.810
Maybe a cloud machine learning
engine, Cloud Functions,

00:40:54.810 --> 00:40:56.190
App Engine--

00:40:56.190 --> 00:40:58.159
pretty much anything
you see on GCP.

00:40:58.159 --> 00:40:58.950
GWEN SHAPIRA: Yeah.

00:40:58.950 --> 00:41:00.533
I haven't shown that,
but I'm actually

00:41:00.533 --> 00:41:06.390
running a Kafka Streams
job running on Google GKE.

00:41:06.390 --> 00:41:08.100
So the Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:41:08.100 --> 00:41:12.060
And that connects to
my Confluent Cloud.

00:41:12.060 --> 00:41:15.335
And as a way to integrate you
apps with the whole Google

00:41:15.335 --> 00:41:17.559
ecosystem.

00:41:17.559 --> 00:41:19.350
JAY SMITH: I think
that's all the questions

00:41:19.350 --> 00:41:21.240
we have for today.

00:41:21.240 --> 00:41:23.032
I'd like to thank you,
Gwen for coming out.

00:41:23.032 --> 00:41:25.031
GWEN SHAPIRA: Thank you
so much for inviting me.

00:41:25.031 --> 00:41:27.450
JAY SMITH: Sharing with the
world everything about Kafka

00:41:27.450 --> 00:41:31.350
and Confluent Cloud and
how it can take your events

00:41:31.350 --> 00:41:34.470
to the next level in the
events that your apps generate.

00:41:34.470 --> 00:41:37.395
And your devices,
those smart switches,

00:41:37.395 --> 00:41:38.920
and cameras and everything.

00:41:38.920 --> 00:41:40.980
GWEN SHAPIRA: And keep
everything real time.

00:41:40.980 --> 00:41:43.220
JAY SMITH: So stay tuned
for the next session.

